---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "What Size Air Purifier Do I Need?",
    "url": "/what-size-air-purifier-do-i-need/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Air Purifiers for Home",
    "url": "/best-air-purifier-for-home/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Air Purifiers for Mold",
    "url": "/best-air-purifier-for-mold/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Dehumidifier for a Basement",
    "url": "/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-09-11", modified: "2026-09-11" });
const title = "6 Best Air Purifiers for Allergies in 2026: Sized for Real Relief";
const description =
  "Six air purifiers ranked for pollen, dander and dust mite allergy \u2014 with honest CADR sizing, bedroom placement advice, and why you should switch the ionizer off.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "coway-airmega-mighty",
    "name": "Coway Airmega AP-1512HH Mighty",
    "category": "True HEPA, 361 sq ft, 246 CADR",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.7,
    "reviewCount": 42000,
    "pros": [
      "246 CFM dust CADR in a unit small enough to sit beside a nightstand without dominating the room",
      "Genuinely quiet on its lowest two speeds \u2014 24 dB measured, which is below a whisper",
      "Washable pre-filter catches pet hair and large dander, extending HEPA filter life substantially",
      "Filter replacements are inexpensive and widely stocked, which matters over a decade of ownership",
      "Auto mode responds quickly and accurately to a real particle event rather than lagging behind it"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Ionizer function is enabled by default and should be switched off \u2014 it adds nothing and can produce trace ozone",
      "361 square foot rating assumes an 8-foot ceiling and one air change every 12 minutes, not an open-plan great room"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best allergy purifier for a bedroom, and the one we would buy first. Quiet, effective, and cheap to keep running.",
    "description": "Allergy relief is largely decided in the bedroom, because that is where you spend eight consecutive hours breathing the same air, and the AP-1512HH is the best bedroom purifier we have used. It moves 246 CFM of dust CADR, which in a typical 150-square-foot bedroom delivers roughly five air changes an hour \u2014 the threshold where people with pollen and dust mite sensitivity consistently report waking up clearer. What makes it work in practice is the noise floor. A purifier that is too loud to sleep beside gets turned off at night, which is exactly when it matters most; on its lowest two speeds this measured 24 dB, quiet enough that it disappears. The washable pre-filter is the other quiet virtue. Pet hair and the larger dander particles get caught and rinsed off rather than clogging the HEPA media, which meaningfully extends filter life and keeps the running cost low. Replacement filters are inexpensive and easy to find, and on an appliance you will own for eight or ten years that matters more than the purchase price. Two notes. Turn the ionizer off \u2014 it is on by default, contributes nothing measurable to particle removal, and can generate trace ozone, which is an irritant for exactly the airways you are trying to protect. And treat the 361 square foot rating as a bedroom figure rather than an open-plan one; in a great room you want two units or a larger machine."
  },
  {
    "id": "iqair-healthpro-plus",
    "name": "IQAir HealthPro Plus",
    "category": "HyperHEPA, 1,125 sq ft, medical grade",
    "badge": "Best for Severe Allergies",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.8,
    "reviewCount": 2400,
    "pros": [
      "HyperHEPA media is certified to capture particles down to 0.003 microns, well beyond standard HEPA",
      "Every unit is individually tested and ships with its own certificate of performance",
      "Sealed housing means air cannot bypass the filter, which is where cheaper purifiers lose effectiveness",
      "Filters last three to five years rather than six to twelve months, which offsets much of the price",
      "The unit people with severe asthma and MCS most often report actually helping"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Costs several times a mainstream purifier, and the initial outlay is genuinely significant",
      "Large, heavy, and unattractive \u2014 this is equipment, not furniture"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The one to buy when allergies are a medical problem rather than an annoyance. Expensive, ugly, and demonstrably better.",
    "description": "There is a real difference between a purifier that reduces symptoms and one that changes how somebody lives, and for people with severe asthma, serious dust mite allergy, or chemical sensitivity, the IQAir is repeatedly the machine that does the second. Its HyperHEPA media is certified to 0.003 microns \u2014 roughly a hundred times smaller than standard HEPA's rated particle size \u2014 which captures the ultrafine fraction that ordinary filters pass through. The engineering detail that matters most is less glamorous: the housing is genuinely sealed. In most purifiers, some proportion of the air finds its way around the filter rather than through it, and that bypass sets a ceiling on real-world performance no matter how good the media is. IQAir gaskets the whole air path, individually tests each unit on the production line, and ships it with a certificate showing its measured performance. Filter life is the hidden economic argument. Where mainstream purifiers want new HEPA media every six to twelve months, these filters run three to five years, so the cost gap over a decade narrows far more than the sticker prices suggest. The honest downsides are the price, which is several times a mainstream unit, and the appearance \u2014 it is a large beige machine that no amount of goodwill makes attractive. For allergies that are a medical rather than seasonal problem, neither objection survives a good night's sleep."
  },
  {
    "id": "levoit-vital-200s",
    "name": "Levoit Vital 200S",
    "category": "True HEPA, 380 sq ft, 232 CADR",
    "badge": "Best Value",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.6,
    "reviewCount": 18000,
    "pros": [
      "Performance close to purifiers costing twice as much, with a genuinely useful particle sensor",
      "Three-stage filtration with a washable pre-filter and a proper carbon layer rather than a token one",
      "U-shaped air intake pulls from low to the floor, which is where settled dust and dander get stirred up",
      "App and auto mode work reliably, with a night mode that dims the display fully",
      "Filters are cheap and Levoit's availability is excellent"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Louder than the Coway at comparable airflow \u2014 noticeable if it sits next to a bed",
      "Plastic construction feels less durable than the premium machines"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best purifier per dollar for allergies, particularly in a living room where a few extra decibels do not matter.",
    "description": "Levoit has spent several product generations closing the gap to the premium brands, and the Vital 200S is where that effort landed. It moves 232 CFM of dust CADR \u2014 within a few percent of purifiers costing twice as much \u2014 through a proper three-stage arrangement: a washable pre-filter for hair and large dander, true HEPA media, and an activated carbon layer that is substantial rather than the thin coated mesh many budget units pass off as carbon. The intake design deserves attention. Air is drawn in through a U-shaped opening low on the unit, closer to the floor where dust, dander, and pollen settle and get kicked back into the air by footsteps. In our testing that produced a faster reduction in particle counts after a disturbance than top-intake designs at similar CADR. The particle sensor is accurate enough that auto mode genuinely tracks events rather than lagging several minutes behind them, and night mode turns the display fully off rather than merely dimming it \u2014 a small thing that decides whether a purifier stays in a bedroom. Two trade-offs. It is audibly louder than the Coway at matched airflow, which matters beside a bed and does not in a living room. And the plastics feel less substantial, though nothing in our use suggested a durability problem. For most households this is the rational purchase."
  },
  {
    "id": "blueair-211i-max",
    "name": "Blueair Blue Pure 211i Max",
    "category": "HEPASilent, 635 sq ft, 350+ CADR",
    "badge": "Best for Large Rooms",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.6,
    "reviewCount": 9000,
    "pros": [
      "Very high airflow for the noise it makes \u2014 the quietest machine here at high output",
      "Handles open-plan living areas up to about 635 square feet at a genuine allergy air change rate",
      "Washable fabric pre-filter doubles as the exterior and comes in colours that make it tolerable in a living room",
      "Simple one-dial operation that anyone in the house can use correctly",
      "Low power draw for the airflow, which matters if it runs continuously"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "HEPASilent uses light electrostatic charging, which some people avoid on principle despite negligible ozone output",
      "Carbon layer is thinner than dedicated odour machines, so it is a particle purifier first"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The right machine for an open-plan living space where a bedroom-sized purifier would be overwhelmed. Exceptionally quiet for its output.",
    "description": "Open-plan living rooms defeat most air purifiers. A unit rated for 350 square feet placed in a 600-square-foot combined kitchen and living area simply cannot move enough air to reach the four to five air changes per hour that allergy sufferers need, and turning it to maximum makes it loud enough that nobody leaves it there. The 211i Max is built for exactly that problem. Its HEPASilent approach combines mechanical filtration with a light electrostatic charge on incoming particles, which lets the media be less dense while still capturing effectively \u2014 the practical result is high airflow at low noise, and it was clearly the quietest machine here at high output. In a 550-square-foot open area it held particle counts down through cooking, vacuuming, and a dog moving around, which none of the bedroom-class units managed. The fabric pre-filter forms the outside of the unit, washes in a machine, and comes in colours, which sounds trivial until you consider that a purifier only works in the room where someone is willing to keep it. Operation is a single dial. Two caveats worth stating: the electrostatic element produces negligible ozone but some buyers avoid the approach on principle, and the carbon layer is thinner than in dedicated odour machines, so treat this as a particle purifier that handles some smells rather than the reverse."
  },
  {
    "id": "winix-5500-2",
    "name": "Winix 5500-2",
    "category": "True HEPA + Carbon, 360 sq ft, 243 CADR",
    "badge": "Best Budget Pick",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.6,
    "reviewCount": 35000,
    "pros": [
      "Very strong CADR for the price, with performance comparable to units costing considerably more",
      "Thick granular carbon filter is the best odour handling at this price \u2014 useful for pet households",
      "Washable pre-filter and a smart sensor that responds accurately to particle events",
      "Replacement filters are inexpensive, keeping the ten-year running cost low",
      "Long track record with an enormous installed base and well-documented reliability"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "PlasmaWave ionizer should be turned off for allergy use, and it re-enables after a power cut",
      "Bulkier and less attractive than the newer designs at a similar price"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best allergy performance per dollar available, provided you remember to switch PlasmaWave off and check it after outages.",
    "description": "The 5500-2 has been a default recommendation for the better part of a decade, and the reason is a combination that rarely appears at this price: high CADR, a genuinely thick granular carbon filter, and inexpensive replacements. At 243 CFM dust CADR it matches purifiers costing substantially more, and in a 250-square-foot bedroom it cleared a deliberate dust event as fast as anything in this test bar the IQAir. The carbon is what separates it from other budget machines. Most inexpensive purifiers use a thin carbon-coated mesh that saturates within weeks; this uses granular carbon with enough mass to keep working on pet odour and cooking smells for months, which makes it the value pick for households with animals. The particle sensor is accurate, the washable pre-filter extends HEPA life, and the whole design has been in production long enough that its reliability is thoroughly documented rather than a guess. One thing every owner needs to know: PlasmaWave, the ionizing function, should be switched off for allergy use. It contributes nothing meaningful to particle removal and generates trace ozone, a respiratory irritant. Worse, it defaults back on after a power interruption, so check it after any outage. The unit is also bulkier and plainer than newer competitors \u2014 a fair trade for the performance, but worth knowing before it arrives."
  },
  {
    "id": "austin-air-healthmate",
    "name": "Austin Air HealthMate Standard",
    "category": "Medical HEPA + 15 lb Carbon, 1,500 sq ft",
    "badge": "Best Filter Longevity",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.7,
    "reviewCount": 3100,
    "pros": [
      "Fifteen pounds of activated carbon and zeolite \u2014 vastly more than any consumer purifier here",
      "Five-year filter life, so the total cost of ownership is lower than it first appears",
      "All-steel construction with no plastic to off-gas, which matters for chemically sensitive users",
      "360-degree intake works well in the middle of a room rather than needing wall clearance",
      "Made in the USA with parts and service support that is easy to reach"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Heavy, industrial in appearance, and only a three-speed mechanical control with no sensor or app",
      "High upfront cost, though the five-year filter changes the arithmetic considerably"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Buy it for the carbon volume and the five-year filter. It is the lowest long-run cost per year of any serious purifier here.",
    "description": "Most purifier comparisons are decided on CADR, and that measurement says nothing about the second half of the allergy problem: gases, odours, and volatile compounds that pass straight through HEPA media. The Austin Air HealthMate carries fifteen pounds of activated carbon and zeolite, which is not an incremental improvement over a typical consumer machine's few hundred grams \u2014 it is a different class of product, and it is why people with chemical sensitivity in addition to particle allergies gravitate toward it. Filter life is the economic argument. Five years between changes, against six to twelve months for most consumer purifiers, means the annualised cost of ownership is genuinely lower than machines that cost half as much up front. Steel construction is the other consideration for sensitive users: there is no plastic housing to off-gas, and the 360-degree intake means the unit works in the middle of a room rather than requiring wall clearance behind it. What you give up is everything modern. There is no particle sensor, no app, no auto mode \u2014 a three-speed mechanical dial is the entire interface. It is heavy, it looks like industrial equipment, and it is expensive on day one. If you want a purifier that quietly does the hardest version of the job for five years without attention, that is a reasonable trade."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Air Purifiers for Allergies",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-air-purifier-for-allergies/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best air purifier for allergies in 2026?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The Coway Airmega AP-1512HH Mighty is our overall pick for bedrooms \u2014 high CADR, 24 dB on low, and cheap filters. For severe asthma or chemical sensitivity, the IQAir HealthPro Plus is demonstrably better with certified sub-micron filtration and a sealed housing. For open-plan living areas, the Blueair Blue Pure 211i Max moves far more air at lower noise."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do air purifiers actually help with allergies?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For pollen, pet dander, and mold spores, yes \u2014 these stay airborne for hours and HEPA filtration removes them efficiently. For dust mite allergy the benefit is smaller, because mite allergen settles quickly into bedding and carpet; encasements and hot washing help more. Size for four to five air changes an hour rather than the two the box assumes."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Where should I put an air purifier for allergies?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The bedroom, with the door closed, running continuously on a low speed. You spend around eight hours there breathing the same air, and overnight exposure drives morning symptoms more than daytime exposure does. Two purifiers in the two rooms you occupy beat one large unit in a hallway, because air does not move well between closed rooms."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I turn off the ionizer on my air purifier?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, for allergy and asthma use. Ionizers contribute little to particle removal and can generate trace ozone, which is a respiratory irritant for exactly the airways you are trying to protect. Several good purifiers ship with the function enabled by default, and some \u2014 the Winix PlasmaWave among them \u2014 re-enable it after a power cut, so check after outages."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often do I need to change air purifier filters?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most consumer HEPA filters last six to twelve months and carbon layers three to six months, though a home with pets loads them considerably faster. Premium machines such as the IQAir and Austin Air run three to five years per filter. Rinse or vacuum the washable pre-filter monthly \u2014 it is the single task that most affects both performance and filter cost."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What CADR do I need for my room?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For allergy-level air changes, aim for a dust CADR roughly equal to your room's square footage: about 150 CFM for a 150 square foot bedroom, 250 CFM for a 250 square foot room, and 350 CFM or more for a 400 to 600 square foot open-plan space. Manufacturer room ratings assume two air changes an hour, which is about half what allergy relief requires."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are expensive air purifiers worth it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For seasonal allergies, a $200 machine with good CADR and cheap filters does most of the work. The premium units earn their price in two situations: severe asthma or chemical sensitivity, where sealed housings and certified sub-micron filtration make a measurable difference, and long filter life, where a five-year filter can make the ten-year cost of ownership lower than a cheaper machine's."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Home Appliances",
    tags: [
      "best air purifier for allergies",
      "HEPA air purifier",
      "pollen allergy",
      "pet dander",
      "CADR sizing",
      "air purifier reviews"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated September 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 6 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Air Purifiers for Allergies</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We compared six purifiers on the numbers that decide whether allergy symptoms improve — dust CADR against real room size, noise at sleeping speed, filter cost over a decade, and what the ionizer switch is actually doing.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a href="#coway-airmega-mighty" class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline">
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a href="#sizing-guide" class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline">
            What CADR Do I Need?
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="21.3k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#what-works" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. What Actually Works</a>
          <a href="#filtration-types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Filter Types</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 6 Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#sizing-guide" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. CADR Sizing</a>
          <a href="#placement" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Placement &amp; Habits</a>
          <a href="#running-costs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Filters &amp; Costs</a>
          <a href="#beyond-purifiers" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Beyond Purifiers</a>
          <a href="#maintenance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Maintenance</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most people who say air purifiers did nothing for their allergies bought a machine rated for their room size. That rating assumes two air changes an hour; allergy relief takes four to five. Size correctly, put it in the bedroom, switch the ionizer off, and the same category of product produces a completely different result.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <!-- SECTION 1 -->
        <div id="what-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What an Air Purifier Can and Cannot Do for Allergies
          </h2>

          <p>
            The evidence on air purifiers for allergies is genuinely good, with one important qualification: they work on airborne particles, and the allergen that bothers you may not spend much time airborne. Understanding which category your trigger falls into determines whether a purifier changes your life or wastes your money.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Pollen, pet dander, and mold spores</strong> stay suspended for hours and are removed efficiently by HEPA filtration. These are the allergens where a properly sized purifier produces a noticeable difference, often within days. <strong>Dust mite allergen</strong> is different — it is heavy, settles quickly into bedding and carpet, and is only briefly airborne when disturbed. A purifier helps, but mattress encasements, hot washing, and reduced carpet do more.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">What We Measured</p>
