          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Placement and Habits That Double the Benefit
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Put it in the bedroom first.</strong> You spend a third of your life there with the door closed, and overnight exposure drives morning symptoms more than daytime exposure does. If you buy one purifier, this is where it goes — not the living room, however much more time it feels like you spend there.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Run it continuously on a low speed</strong> rather than intermittently on high. Steady operation holds particle counts down; intermittent operation lets them rebuild between sessions, and the recovery takes far longer than people expect. A quiet machine running all night beats a powerful one you switch off to sleep.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Give it clearance.</strong> Most units draw from the front or sides and exhaust upward, and shoving one against a wall or into a corner restricts intake enough to lose a meaningful share of its rated airflow. A foot of space around it is usually sufficient.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Close the windows during pollen season.</strong> A purifier cannot keep up with an open window during a high pollen count, and running one in a room with outdoor air pouring in simply wears out the filter. The purifier's job is to clean the air that is already inside and the pollen you bring in on clothes and hair.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Shower before bed during pollen season.</strong> Pollen collects in hair and on skin during the day and transfers straight onto the pillow, where you then breathe it for eight hours. This costs nothing and helps more than most equipment purchases.
          </p>
        </div>

