        <!-- SECTION 7 -->
        <div id="beyond-purifiers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Helps More Than a Second Purifier
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once one good purifier is running in the bedroom, the next dollar is usually better spent elsewhere. For dust mite allergy, zippered mattress and pillow encasements are the single highest-value intervention available, because they put a barrier between you and the largest reservoir of allergen in the house. Washing bedding weekly at 130 degrees F or above kills mites; cooler washes do not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Humidity control is the other lever. Dust mites and mold both need moisture, and holding indoor relative humidity between 40 and 50 percent suppresses both. In a damp basement or a humid climate, a dehumidifier often does more for symptoms than a second purifier would — our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement dehumidifier guide</a> covers sizing for that.
          </p>

          <p>
            For pet allergies, the honest advice is unglamorous: keep the animal out of the bedroom. Dander accumulates in bedding and soft furnishings, and no purifier can compete with a cat sleeping on the pillow. Combine that with a HEPA vacuum and weekly washing of anything the animal lies on, and the improvement is larger than any equipment upgrade.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, if your HVAC system runs regularly, fitting a MERV 13 filter turns the whole house into a filtration system when the blower runs. Check that your system can handle the pressure drop first — older furnaces sometimes cannot — but where it works, it is inexpensive and covers rooms no purifier is in.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8 -->
        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Maintenance People Skip
          </h2>

          <p>
            Vacuum or rinse the pre-filter monthly. This is the task that most affects both performance and filter cost, and it takes two minutes. A pre-filter thick with pet hair chokes airflow, which lowers the effective CADR of the machine well below what you paid for.
          </p>

          <p>
            Replace HEPA media on schedule rather than when the indicator light insists. Most indicators are timers rather than sensors, and they are approximately right — but a purifier in a home with two dogs loads its filter far faster than the same unit in a low-dust apartment. If airflow feels lower and the pre-filter is clean, the HEPA is loaded.
          </p>

          <p>
            Never wash a HEPA filter unless the manufacturer explicitly says it is washable. Water damages the fibre structure and destroys the electrostatic charge many HEPA media rely on, leaving a filter that looks clean and performs badly. Carbon filters cannot be regenerated at home either — once saturated they need replacing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep a spare filter on hand before the season starts. Pollen counts spike on a schedule, filter stock does not, and a purifier waiting three weeks for a delivery during peak season is doing nothing for the person who needs it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, wipe the sensor window if your unit has an auto mode. Dust accumulates on the optical particle sensor and makes it read high, which leaves the fan running at full speed permanently and wears the machine out for nothing.
          </p>
        </div>


