          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best air purifier for allergies in 2026?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Coway Airmega AP-1512HH Mighty is our overall pick for bedrooms — high CADR, 24 dB on low, and cheap filters. For severe asthma or chemical sensitivity, the IQAir HealthPro Plus is demonstrably better with certified sub-micron filtration and a sealed housing. For open-plan living areas, the Blueair Blue Pure 211i Max moves far more air at lower noise.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do air purifiers actually help with allergies?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For pollen, pet dander, and mold spores, yes — these stay airborne for hours and HEPA filtration removes them efficiently. For dust mite allergy the benefit is smaller, because mite allergen settles quickly into bedding and carpet; encasements and hot washing help more. Size for four to five air changes an hour rather than the two the box assumes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where should I put an air purifier for allergies?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The bedroom, with the door closed, running continuously on a low speed. You spend around eight hours there breathing the same air, and overnight exposure drives morning symptoms more than daytime exposure does. Two purifiers in the two rooms you occupy beat one large unit in a hallway, because air does not move well between closed rooms.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I turn off the ionizer on my air purifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for allergy and asthma use. Ionizers contribute little to particle removal and can generate trace ozone, which is a respiratory irritant for exactly the airways you are trying to protect. Several good purifiers ship with the function enabled by default, and some — the Winix PlasmaWave among them — re-enable it after a power cut, so check after outages.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often do I need to change air purifier filters?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most consumer HEPA filters last six to twelve months and carbon layers three to six months, though a home with pets loads them considerably faster. Premium machines such as the IQAir and Austin Air run three to five years per filter. Rinse or vacuum the washable pre-filter monthly — it is the single task that most affects both performance and filter cost.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What CADR do I need for my room?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For allergy-level air changes, aim for a dust CADR roughly equal to your room's square footage: about 150 CFM for a 150 square foot bedroom, 250 CFM for a 250 square foot room, and 350 CFM or more for a 400 to 600 square foot open-plan space. Manufacturer room ratings assume two air changes an hour, which is about half what allergy relief requires.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are expensive air purifiers worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For seasonal allergies, a $200 machine with good CADR and cheap filters does most of the work. The premium units earn their price in two situations: severe asthma or chemical sensitivity, where sealed housings and certified sub-micron filtration make a measurable difference, and long filter life, where a five-year filter can make the ten-year cost of ownership lower than a cheaper machine's.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Air Purifier Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every one of these is a decision made before the machine is plugged in, and every one of them limits how much relief you get.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing to the Box Rather Than to Air Changes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Manufacturer room ratings assume two air changes an hour, and allergy relief needs four to five. Buy a purifier rated for roughly twice the room you are putting it in, or accept that you will have to run it on a noisy high speed to get the benefit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting the Only Purifier in the Living Room</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The bedroom is where you spend eight uninterrupted hours behind a closed door, and it is where overnight exposure sets up morning symptoms. If you own one purifier, it belongs beside the bed. Air does not circulate between closed rooms well enough for a hallway unit to help.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the Ionizer On</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ionizing functions add little to particle capture and can produce trace ozone, an irritant for allergic and asthmatic airways. Switch it off, and check it again after any power interruption because several models silently default back on.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on Price Without Checking Filter Costs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A cheap purifier with $90 filters twice a year costs more over ten years than an expensive one with a five-year filter. Look up the replacement price and availability before you buy the machine, especially with brands you have not heard of.
